{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a lampstand of pure gold.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the purpose of the lampstand in the Tabernacle and in the Temple was to establish in the soul the preeminence of the House and its awe, and so that the fear of that place be fixed in the hearts, engraved in the thoughts, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 19:30) ״ומקדשי תיראו״. From this it was required to be outside the curtain and not within the curtain, as it is written (Leviticus 24:3) ״מחוץ לפרכת העדות באהל מועד יערוך״ etc., so that the matter be seen and publicly proclaimed to everyone coming and going, and the multitudes would see and fear. And furthermore, to show that He does not need our light; and therefore it was of gold, which is the finest of all metals, and the lamps were seven, which is an intermediate number, being a middle measure." "<b>And know</b> that the soul takes pleasure in the kindling of the lamps, and it walks in the delights of splendor and joy, and expands and widens through the pleasure of the light, because it is a piece of light hewn from the light of the intellect. And for this reason it is drawn after light, which is of its own kind, even though it is physical light while the soul is spiritual light, pure and simple. And therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, likened it to a lamp, as he said (Proverbs 20:27) ״נר ה׳ נשמת אדם״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: the lampstand with its seven lamps corresponds to the seven stars that course through all the earth — thus they expounded in Tanhuma. And the explanation of this is that the seven wandering stars are lamps in the heavens, and they act upon the lower realm and govern this lower world continually, day and night; likewise the seven lamps burned day and night before HaShem continually. And the central lamp corresponds to the sphere of the sun, which is constant in its light and is central among the seven wandering stars, according to the manner of צ״ם חנכ״ל. And likewise the fourth day is the middle of the seven days, and therefore the sun was created on the fourth day." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the lampstand with its seven lamps alludes to the Torah, which is called light, as it is said (Proverbs 6:23) ״כי נר מצוה ותורה אור״, and it encompasses seven wisdoms, and therefore its lamps were seven. And the six branches coming out from its sides correspond to the six extremities of the world, which depend upon the Torah that was given on the sixth of Sivan, for the world is sustained on its account, as it is said (Jeremiah 33) ״אם לא בריתי יומם ולילה״. Three goblet-shaped cups (גביעים) on one branch — in each branch there were three cups; if so, that makes eighteen cups for the six branches, and in the lampstand itself four other cups, making twenty-two cups; and they correspond to the twenty-two letters of the Torah and to the twenty-two parts of the world, which are: wind and water, fire, the seven wandering stars, the twelve constellations; and they correspond to the twenty-two parts of the human body: the head, the heart, the kidneys, the seven orifices of the head, and twelve limbs. And we find in the lampstand three things, namely: knob (כפתור), cup (גביע), and flower (פרח). The כפתור is a round apple-shaped knob protruding and going around the central branch of the lampstand. The כפתור corresponds to the world of the angels, who delight in the splendor of the Shekhinah with an endless and boundless delight; and this is the כפתור, which is round, for the round has neither beginning nor end. And of this delight the Sages of truth, may their memory be for a blessing, hinted in their midrash: the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make a dance (מחול) for the righteous in the World to Come, and His glory is among them — for the dance is round and has neither beginning nor end; and therefore they likened the delight of the souls, which has no end, to the dance, which has no end. The גביע is a vessel that has a receiving-space, and it gives drink and waters — corresponding to the world of the spheres, which receive the forces of the supernal realms and bestow force upon the lower world. The פרח corresponds to this lower world, where all the great ones of the earth — plants and flowers and blossoms — are; and this comes to hint that through the twenty-two letters of the Torah, a person attains the three parts of existence."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The kabbalistic tag צ״ם חנכ״ל is left untranslated per the rule that way-of-the-Kabbalah material stays cryptic; no expansion or decoding is provided."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:38.146467083Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}